# Universal Player Display Standards

1. All player-facing game interfaces must be designed mobile-first and meet professional responsive application standards.
2. Every player must have access to the same gameplay information, controls, and functionality intended for them, regardless of screen size, device type, or orientation.
3. Layouts must adapt intelligently rather than hide, truncate, overlap, or discard important content.
4. Prioritize fitting active gameplay within the viewport. Allow vertical scrolling when genuinely necessary, but never require horizontal page scrolling.
5. Support phones, tablets, foldables, and desktops in both portrait and landscape orientations.
6. Maintain readable text, accessible contrast, and touch targets of at least 44x44 CSS pixels.
7. Respect device safe areas, browser toolbars, notches, and dynamic viewport dimensions.
8. Never rely on hover, device-specific features, or a particular orientation for essential gameplay functionality.
9. Test responsive layouts at widths of 320, 360, 375, 390, and 430 pixels, plus tablet and desktop sizes.
10. Whenever modifying a player-facing interface, check for overflow, clipping, inaccessible controls, and information loss.
11. When screen space is insufficient, redesign the layout rather than remove functionality.
12. Do not consider player-facing UI work complete without responsive validation.

This rule applies to all existing and future player-facing screens in this project.
